1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,973
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:02,153 --> 00:00:04,777
Here's something most money advice gets wrong.

3
00:00:04,957 --> 00:00:06,859
It assumes people are broke because they

4
00:00:06,859 --> 00:00:08,428
don't know the right information.

5
00:00:08,608 --> 00:00:09,920
Spend less than you earn.

6
00:00:10,100 --> 00:00:11,076
Avoid debt.

7
00:00:11,256 --> 00:00:12,242
Invest early.

8
00:00:12,422 --> 00:00:13,572
We all know that.

9
00:00:13,752 --> 00:00:15,432
And yet millions of smart,

10
00:00:15,432 --> 00:00:17,630
hardworking people still struggle.

11
00:00:17,810 --> 00:00:18,030
Why?

12
00:00:18,210 --> 00:00:20,741
Because the problem usually isn't knowledge.

13
00:00:20,921 --> 00:00:22,001
It's psychology.

14
00:00:22,181 --> 00:00:24,514
Your brain has built in shortcuts that

15
00:00:24,514 --> 00:00:26,295
work great for everyday life,

16
00:00:26,295 --> 00:00:27,707
and terribly for money.

17
00:00:27,887 --> 00:00:30,441
Today, twenty of those mental traps,

18
00:00:30,441 --> 00:00:32,357
and how to escape each one.

19
00:00:32,706 --> 00:00:34,573
Here's a study that changes how you

20
00:00:34,573 --> 00:00:35,853
think about being broke.

21
00:00:36,033 --> 00:00:39,131
Researchers published in the journal Science found

22
00:00:39,131 --> 00:00:41,423
that the same farmers in India scored

23
00:00:41,423 --> 00:00:44,583
noticeably worse on cognitive tests before harvest,

24
00:00:44,583 --> 00:00:45,884
when money was tight,

25
00:00:45,884 --> 00:00:47,061
than after harvest,

26
00:00:47,061 --> 00:00:48,548
when they had been paid.

27
00:00:48,899 --> 00:00:50,350
Think about what that means.

28
00:00:50,530 --> 00:00:51,513
The same people,

29
00:00:51,513 --> 00:00:53,173
with the same intelligence,

30
00:00:53,173 --> 00:00:56,000
performed worse on mental tests simply because

31
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:57,844
they were worried about money.

32
00:00:58,024 --> 00:01:01,081
The researchers argued that financial stress takes

33
00:01:01,081 --> 00:01:02,304
up mental bandwidth,

34
00:01:02,304 --> 00:01:04,688
leaving less room for careful thinking.

35
00:01:04,868 --> 00:01:05,844
In other words,

36
00:01:05,844 --> 00:01:08,316
being broke doesn't just come from bad

37
00:01:08,316 --> 00:01:08,966
decisions.

38
00:01:09,146 --> 00:01:10,540
It can also cause them.

39
00:01:10,720 --> 00:01:13,332
That's one of the most important ideas

40
00:01:13,332 --> 00:01:14,776
in this entire video,

41
00:01:14,776 --> 00:01:17,320
and it's why willpower alone so often

42
00:01:17,320 --> 00:01:17,732
fails.

43
00:01:18,082 --> 00:01:20,431
We'll go through these twenty traps from

44
00:01:20,431 --> 00:01:21,194
three angles.

45
00:01:21,374 --> 00:01:22,848
First, the numbers.

46
00:01:23,028 --> 00:01:25,262
The ways our brains get money math

47
00:01:25,262 --> 00:01:27,759
wrong, from big interest rates to tiny

48
00:01:27,759 --> 00:01:28,415
purchases.

49
00:01:28,595 --> 00:01:30,523
Second, the psychology.

50
00:01:30,703 --> 00:01:32,756
The emotional traps around fear,

51
00:01:32,756 --> 00:01:34,360
comparison and avoidance.

52
00:01:34,540 --> 00:01:36,200
And third, the playbook.

53
00:01:36,380 --> 00:01:38,263
The habits of mind that keep us

54
00:01:38,263 --> 00:01:40,875
stuck, along with the research backed tools

55
00:01:40,875 --> 00:01:42,940
that actually work to get unstuck.

56
00:01:43,120 --> 00:01:45,291
None of this is about blaming anyone.

57
00:01:45,471 --> 00:01:48,116
It's about understanding the machine between your

58
00:01:48,116 --> 00:01:49,682
ears, so you can work with it

59
00:01:49,682 --> 00:01:50,869
instead of against it.

60
00:01:51,219 --> 00:01:52,944
Let's begin with the numbers,

61
00:01:52,944 --> 00:01:54,965
and a trap that costs people money

62
00:01:54,965 --> 00:01:56,630
every single time they shop.

63
00:01:56,979 --> 00:02:00,170
Our sources are published research in behavioral

64
00:02:00,170 --> 00:02:01,832
economics and psychology.

65
00:02:02,012 --> 00:02:05,246
Work by Nobel Prize winners Daniel Kahneman

66
00:02:05,246 --> 00:02:06,674
and Richard Thaler,

67
00:02:06,674 --> 00:02:10,358
research by Sendhil Mullainathan and Eldar Shafir

68
00:02:10,358 --> 00:02:13,216
on scarcity, studies by Drazen Prelec,

69
00:02:13,216 --> 00:02:16,223
Dan Ariely, Brigitte Madrian and others.

70
00:02:16,403 --> 00:02:18,284
I'll name each study as we go.

71
00:02:18,464 --> 00:02:21,726
Some famous psychology findings have had trouble

72
00:02:21,726 --> 00:02:24,988
replicating, so I've focused on well established

73
00:02:24,988 --> 00:02:27,706
ones, and I'll flag uncertainty where it

74
00:02:27,706 --> 00:02:28,181
exists.

75
00:02:28,361 --> 00:02:31,318
Growth examples use a stated seven percent

76
00:02:31,318 --> 00:02:32,867
average yearly return,

77
00:02:32,867 --> 00:02:35,049
an illustration, not a promise.

78
00:02:35,229 --> 00:02:36,542
This is education,

79
00:02:36,542 --> 00:02:38,073
not financial advice.

80
00:02:38,423 --> 00:02:39,155
Angle one.

81
00:02:39,335 --> 00:02:40,124
The numbers.

82
00:02:40,304 --> 00:02:42,549
Seven ways your brain gets money math

83
00:02:42,549 --> 00:02:44,612
wrong, often without you noticing.

84
00:02:44,961 --> 00:02:46,088
Trap number one.

85
00:02:46,268 --> 00:02:46,860
Anchoring.

86
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:49,180
The first number you see shapes every

87
00:02:49,180 --> 00:02:50,105
number after it.

88
00:02:50,455 --> 00:02:51,993
In a famous experiment,

89
00:02:51,993 --> 00:02:54,601
Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman spun a

90
00:02:54,601 --> 00:02:57,143
wheel of fortune that landed on either

91
00:02:57,143 --> 00:02:58,681
a low or a high number,

92
00:02:58,681 --> 00:03:01,490
then asked people to estimate a completely

93
00:03:01,490 --> 00:03:02,761
unrelated quantity.

94
00:03:02,941 --> 00:03:05,256
People who saw the higher number gave

95
00:03:05,256 --> 00:03:06,633
much higher estimates.

96
00:03:06,813 --> 00:03:07,827
A random number,

97
00:03:07,827 --> 00:03:10,045
with no connection to the question,

98
00:03:10,045 --> 00:03:11,376
pulled their answers.

99
00:03:11,556 --> 00:03:13,548
That's anchoring, and stores,

100
00:03:13,548 --> 00:03:16,296
car dealers and salespeople use it every

101
00:03:16,296 --> 00:03:16,571
day.

102
00:03:16,921 --> 00:03:19,353
Think of the original price crossed out

103
00:03:19,353 --> 00:03:20,788
next to the sale price.

104
00:03:20,968 --> 00:03:23,603
Or the expensive model placed beside the

105
00:03:23,603 --> 00:03:25,778
one the store really wants you to

106
00:03:25,778 --> 00:03:26,041
buy.

107
00:03:26,221 --> 00:03:28,236
Or the car's sticker price that makes

108
00:03:28,236 --> 00:03:30,250
the negotiated price feel like a win.

109
00:03:30,430 --> 00:03:32,434
The anchor makes a high price seem

110
00:03:32,434 --> 00:03:34,261
reasonable, even when it isn't.

111
00:03:34,441 --> 00:03:36,360
You leave feeling you saved money,

112
00:03:36,360 --> 00:03:38,336
when you may have simply spent less

113
00:03:38,336 --> 00:03:40,142
than an inflated starting point.

114
00:03:40,492 --> 00:03:41,293
Escape it.

115
00:03:41,473 --> 00:03:42,475
Before you shop,

116
00:03:42,475 --> 00:03:43,916
decide your own number,

117
00:03:43,916 --> 00:03:46,046
based on your budget and research,

118
00:03:46,046 --> 00:03:47,173
and write it down.

119
00:03:47,353 --> 00:03:50,023
Ignore the crossed out price completely.

120
00:03:50,203 --> 00:03:52,158
Ask yourself, would I pay this if

121
00:03:52,158 --> 00:03:54,290
I had never seen the original price?

122
00:03:54,470 --> 00:03:56,177
If not, it's not a deal.

123
00:03:56,357 --> 00:03:57,344
It's an anchor.

124
00:03:57,694 --> 00:03:58,994
Trap number two.

125
00:03:59,174 --> 00:04:00,254
Mental accounting.

126
00:04:00,434 --> 00:04:02,766
Treating some dollars as less real than

127
00:04:02,766 --> 00:04:03,185
others.

128
00:04:03,536 --> 00:04:05,997
Richard Thaler, who won the Nobel Prize

129
00:04:05,997 --> 00:04:08,775
in economics, described how people put money

130
00:04:08,775 --> 00:04:10,037
into mental buckets.

131
00:04:10,217 --> 00:04:12,690
Money from a paycheck feels serious.

132
00:04:12,870 --> 00:04:14,683
Money from a tax refund,

133
00:04:14,683 --> 00:04:16,874
a bonus or a casino win feels

134
00:04:16,874 --> 00:04:18,083
like play money.

135
00:04:18,263 --> 00:04:20,817
Rationally, every dollar is the same.

136
00:04:20,997 --> 00:04:23,571
But emotionally, we spend found money much

137
00:04:23,571 --> 00:04:24,306
more easily.

138
00:04:24,656 --> 00:04:27,091
This is why tax refunds often disappear

139
00:04:27,091 --> 00:04:29,463
quickly, and why people can keep money

140
00:04:29,463 --> 00:04:31,898
in a low interest savings account while

141
00:04:31,898 --> 00:04:34,396
carrying a credit card balance at twenty

142
00:04:34,396 --> 00:04:34,896
percent.

143
00:04:35,076 --> 00:04:37,525
The savings bucket feels untouchable,

144
00:04:37,525 --> 00:04:39,975
while the debt bucket feels separate.

145
00:04:40,155 --> 00:04:42,543
In reality, using the savings to pay

146
00:04:42,543 --> 00:04:44,069
off the expensive debt,

147
00:04:44,069 --> 00:04:46,391
while keeping an emergency cushion,

148
00:04:46,391 --> 00:04:48,979
would often leave them much better off.

149
00:04:49,329 --> 00:04:50,095
Escape it.

150
00:04:50,275 --> 00:04:52,550
Treat every dollar as the same dollar.

151
00:04:52,730 --> 00:04:54,391
Before a windfall arrives,

152
00:04:54,391 --> 00:04:56,881
decide in writing exactly where it will

153
00:04:56,881 --> 00:04:57,072
go.

154
00:04:57,252 --> 00:04:59,653
And look at your whole financial picture

155
00:04:59,653 --> 00:05:01,813
at once, savings and debts together,

156
00:05:01,813 --> 00:05:03,673
so the buckets don't trick you.

157
00:05:04,023 --> 00:05:05,427
Trap number three.

158
00:05:05,607 --> 00:05:06,583
Present bias.

159
00:05:06,763 --> 00:05:09,561
Today always feels more important than tomorrow.

160
00:05:09,911 --> 00:05:12,912
Economists have shown that people value rewards

161
00:05:12,912 --> 00:05:14,955
now far more than rewards later,

162
00:05:14,955 --> 00:05:17,126
even when the later reward is much

163
00:05:17,126 --> 00:05:17,573
bigger.

164
00:05:17,753 --> 00:05:19,965
Ask people whether they want one hundred

165
00:05:19,965 --> 00:05:22,177
dollars today or one hundred ten dollars

166
00:05:22,177 --> 00:05:22,730
in a week,

167
00:05:22,730 --> 00:05:24,278
and many take the money now.

168
00:05:24,458 --> 00:05:26,484
But ask about one hundred dollars in

169
00:05:26,484 --> 00:05:28,285
a year versus one hundred ten in

170
00:05:28,285 --> 00:05:29,298
a year and a week,

171
00:05:29,298 --> 00:05:30,367
and most will wait.

172
00:05:30,547 --> 00:05:32,165
The extra week is the same,

173
00:05:32,165 --> 00:05:33,903
but the present pulls harder.

174
00:05:34,253 --> 00:05:37,051
With money, present bias is why saving

175
00:05:37,051 --> 00:05:39,187
for retirement feels so hard.

176
00:05:39,367 --> 00:05:41,132
Retirement is decades away,

177
00:05:41,132 --> 00:05:43,094
and the weekend is right here.

178
00:05:43,274 --> 00:05:45,366
It's also why buy now pay later

179
00:05:45,366 --> 00:05:47,592
and credit cards are so tempting.

180
00:05:47,772 --> 00:05:49,990
They let you enjoy the present and

181
00:05:49,990 --> 00:05:52,077
push the cost into a future that

182
00:05:52,077 --> 00:05:53,055
feels abstract.

183
00:05:53,405 --> 00:05:54,124
Escape it.

184
00:05:54,304 --> 00:05:55,860
Make the future feel real.

185
00:05:56,040 --> 00:05:58,682
Some research suggests that people who view

186
00:05:58,682 --> 00:06:01,508
age progressed photos of themselves save more.

187
00:06:01,688 --> 00:06:03,504
You can also take the decision out

188
00:06:03,504 --> 00:06:04,786
of the present entirely,

189
00:06:04,786 --> 00:06:07,296
with automatic transfers that happen before you

190
00:06:07,296 --> 00:06:08,097
can be tempted.

191
00:06:08,447 --> 00:06:09,631
Trap number four.

192
00:06:09,811 --> 00:06:11,564
Exponential growth bias.

193
00:06:11,744 --> 00:06:14,461
We underestimate how fast things compound.

194
00:06:14,811 --> 00:06:18,225
Economists Victor Stango and Jonathan Zinman studied

195
00:06:18,225 --> 00:06:20,917
what they called exponential growth bias.

196
00:06:21,097 --> 00:06:23,356
Most people think of growth in straight

197
00:06:23,356 --> 00:06:25,614
lines, but compound interest grows in a

198
00:06:25,614 --> 00:06:25,962
curve.

199
00:06:26,142 --> 00:06:28,838
So people tend to underestimate how quickly

200
00:06:28,838 --> 00:06:31,032
savings can grow over long periods,

201
00:06:31,032 --> 00:06:33,038
and how quickly debt can grow at

202
00:06:33,038 --> 00:06:34,292
high interest rates.

203
00:06:34,643 --> 00:06:35,653
Here's an example.

204
00:06:35,833 --> 00:06:37,775
One hundred dollars a month at a

205
00:06:37,775 --> 00:06:40,507
seven percent average yearly return for forty

206
00:06:40,507 --> 00:06:40,871
years.

207
00:06:41,051 --> 00:06:43,341
Most people guess well under one hundred

208
00:06:43,341 --> 00:06:44,314
thousand dollars.

209
00:06:44,494 --> 00:06:47,111
The actual result is roughly two hundred

210
00:06:47,111 --> 00:06:48,093
sixty thousand.

211
00:06:48,273 --> 00:06:49,325
On the debt side,

212
00:06:49,325 --> 00:06:52,110
people underestimate how much a high interest

213
00:06:52,110 --> 00:06:54,337
balance grows when they only pay the

214
00:06:54,337 --> 00:06:54,832
minimum.

215
00:06:55,012 --> 00:06:57,616
The research found that people with stronger

216
00:06:57,616 --> 00:07:00,279
exponential growth bias tended to borrow more

217
00:07:00,279 --> 00:07:01,108
and save less.

218
00:07:01,458 --> 00:07:02,177
Escape it.

219
00:07:02,357 --> 00:07:03,414
Stop guessing.

220
00:07:03,594 --> 00:07:06,514
Use a compound interest calculator for both

221
00:07:06,514 --> 00:07:07,669
savings and debt.

222
00:07:07,849 --> 00:07:09,328
Seeing the actual curve,

223
00:07:09,328 --> 00:07:11,238
on screen, is the best cure for

224
00:07:11,238 --> 00:07:12,656
straight line thinking.

225
00:07:13,006 --> 00:07:14,039
Here's that curve.

226
00:07:14,219 --> 00:07:16,023
One hundred dollars a month,

227
00:07:16,023 --> 00:07:17,054
for forty years,

228
00:07:17,054 --> 00:07:19,696
at a seven percent average yearly return,

229
00:07:19,696 --> 00:07:22,144
grows to roughly two hundred sixty two

230
00:07:22,144 --> 00:07:23,240
thousand dollars.

231
00:07:23,420 --> 00:07:25,196
You'd put in forty eight thousand.

232
00:07:25,546 --> 00:07:26,962
Trap number five.

233
00:07:27,142 --> 00:07:28,965
The sunk cost fallacy.

234
00:07:29,145 --> 00:07:31,585
Sticking with something because you've already paid

235
00:07:31,585 --> 00:07:31,919
for it.

236
00:07:32,270 --> 00:07:35,248
Researchers Hal Arkes and Catherine Blumer showed

237
00:07:35,248 --> 00:07:38,227
that people often continue investing in something

238
00:07:38,227 --> 00:07:40,963
simply because they've already put money into

239
00:07:40,963 --> 00:07:43,334
it, even when quitting would be better.

240
00:07:43,514 --> 00:07:46,252
Money that's already spent and can't be

241
00:07:46,252 --> 00:07:48,007
recovered is a sunk cost.

242
00:07:48,187 --> 00:07:51,287
Rationally, it shouldn't affect future decisions.

243
00:07:51,467 --> 00:07:53,301
Emotionally, it does.

244
00:07:53,652 --> 00:07:55,823
Think of repairing an old car again

245
00:07:55,823 --> 00:07:58,368
and again because you've already spent so

246
00:07:58,368 --> 00:07:59,050
much on it.

247
00:07:59,230 --> 00:08:01,809
Or holding a losing investment because selling

248
00:08:01,809 --> 00:08:03,491
would make the loss feel real.

249
00:08:03,671 --> 00:08:05,613
Or finishing a meal you don't enjoy

250
00:08:05,613 --> 00:08:06,945
because you paid for it.

251
00:08:07,125 --> 00:08:08,033
In each case,

252
00:08:08,033 --> 00:08:10,755
the past payment pushes you toward more

253
00:08:10,755 --> 00:08:12,361
spending, or more loss.

254
00:08:12,712 --> 00:08:13,385
Escape it.

255
00:08:13,565 --> 00:08:14,949
When making a decision,

256
00:08:14,949 --> 00:08:16,514
ask only about the future.

257
00:08:16,694 --> 00:08:18,534
Ignoring what I've already spent,

258
00:08:18,534 --> 00:08:20,374
what's the best choice from here?

259
00:08:20,554 --> 00:08:22,372
If you were starting fresh today,

260
00:08:22,372 --> 00:08:23,584
would you choose this?

261
00:08:23,764 --> 00:08:25,533
If not, it may be time to

262
00:08:25,533 --> 00:08:26,028
let go.

263
00:08:26,379 --> 00:08:27,818
Trap number six.

264
00:08:27,998 --> 00:08:29,949
Painless payments make you spend more.

265
00:08:30,299 --> 00:08:32,948
Drazen Prelec and Duncan Simester ran a

266
00:08:32,948 --> 00:08:35,666
famous study where people bid on tickets

267
00:08:35,666 --> 00:08:37,636
to sold out basketball games.

268
00:08:37,816 --> 00:08:39,679
Those told they had to pay by

269
00:08:39,679 --> 00:08:42,569
credit card were willing to pay substantially

270
00:08:42,569 --> 00:08:44,689
more, in some cases about double,

271
00:08:44,689 --> 00:08:46,744
compared with those paying cash.

272
00:08:46,924 --> 00:08:49,498
Paying with cash creates a small feeling

273
00:08:49,498 --> 00:08:50,012
of pain.

274
00:08:50,192 --> 00:08:52,965
Cards, and especially tapping a phone,

275
00:08:52,965 --> 00:08:54,279
remove most of it.

276
00:08:54,629 --> 00:08:57,861
Today, almost everything is designed to remove

277
00:08:57,861 --> 00:08:58,564
that pain.

278
00:08:58,744 --> 00:09:00,106
One click checkout,

279
00:09:00,106 --> 00:09:03,831
saved cards, subscriptions that renew automatically,

280
00:09:03,831 --> 00:09:04,619
tap to pay.

281
00:09:04,799 --> 00:09:07,111
Each one makes spending feel lighter,

282
00:09:07,111 --> 00:09:08,236
and more frequent.

283
00:09:08,416 --> 00:09:10,157
It's not that cards are evil.

284
00:09:10,337 --> 00:09:12,334
It's that they quietly remove one of

285
00:09:12,334 --> 00:09:13,832
the brain's natural brakes.

286
00:09:14,182 --> 00:09:14,971
Escape it.

287
00:09:15,151 --> 00:09:16,358
Add friction back.

288
00:09:16,538 --> 00:09:19,116
Delete saved cards from shopping sites.

289
00:09:19,296 --> 00:09:22,099
Use cash for categories where you tend

290
00:09:22,099 --> 00:09:24,311
to overspend, like eating out.

291
00:09:24,491 --> 00:09:26,571
And check your card balance often,

292
00:09:26,571 --> 00:09:28,590
so the spending feels real again.

293
00:09:28,940 --> 00:09:30,460
Trap number seven.

294
00:09:30,640 --> 00:09:32,530
The word free makes us lose our

295
00:09:32,530 --> 00:09:33,079
judgment.

296
00:09:33,429 --> 00:09:36,278
In experiments by Dan Ariely and colleagues,

297
00:09:36,278 --> 00:09:39,256
people chose between a fancy chocolate truffle

298
00:09:39,256 --> 00:09:41,134
for a small price and a cheap

299
00:09:41,134 --> 00:09:42,624
chocolate for one cent.

300
00:09:42,804 --> 00:09:44,116
Many chose the truffle.

301
00:09:44,296 --> 00:09:46,437
But when both prices dropped by one

302
00:09:46,437 --> 00:09:48,394
cent, making the cheap one free,

303
00:09:48,394 --> 00:09:51,147
people switched dramatically to the free one,

304
00:09:51,147 --> 00:09:53,777
even though the price difference stayed the

305
00:09:53,777 --> 00:09:54,083
same.

306
00:09:54,263 --> 00:09:56,132
Zero isn't just a low price.

307
00:09:56,312 --> 00:09:58,367
It feels like a different category.

308
00:09:58,717 --> 00:10:00,621
Businesses use this constantly.

309
00:10:00,801 --> 00:10:03,074
Free shipping if you spend thirty five

310
00:10:03,074 --> 00:10:03,552
dollars.

311
00:10:03,732 --> 00:10:05,253
Buy one, get one free.

312
00:10:05,433 --> 00:10:07,537
A free trial that turns into a

313
00:10:07,537 --> 00:10:08,800
paid subscription.

314
00:10:08,980 --> 00:10:10,721
Free gifts with purchase.

315
00:10:10,901 --> 00:10:12,772
Often, the free part leads us to

316
00:10:12,772 --> 00:10:14,350
spend more than we planned.

317
00:10:14,699 --> 00:10:15,524
Escape it.

318
00:10:15,704 --> 00:10:16,758
When you see free,

319
00:10:16,758 --> 00:10:18,281
ask what it's costing you.

320
00:10:18,461 --> 00:10:20,294
Did you add items just to qualify

321
00:10:20,294 --> 00:10:21,294
for free shipping?

322
00:10:21,474 --> 00:10:23,462
Will you remember to cancel that free

323
00:10:23,462 --> 00:10:23,784
trial?

324
00:10:23,964 --> 00:10:25,940
Put a reminder in your calendar the

325
00:10:25,940 --> 00:10:27,633
day you sign up for any trial.

326
00:10:27,983 --> 00:10:29,985
You might think these traps only fool

327
00:10:29,985 --> 00:10:30,851
careless people.

328
00:10:31,031 --> 00:10:31,681
They don't.

329
00:10:31,861 --> 00:10:34,648
Kahneman described two systems of thinking.

330
00:10:34,828 --> 00:10:36,182
System one is fast,

331
00:10:36,182 --> 00:10:37,893
automatic and emotional.

332
00:10:38,073 --> 00:10:39,617
System two is slow,

333
00:10:39,617 --> 00:10:41,242
careful and logical.

334
00:10:41,422 --> 00:10:43,021
Most daily decisions,

335
00:10:43,021 --> 00:10:44,847
including most spending,

336
00:10:44,847 --> 00:10:46,217
run on system one.

337
00:10:46,397 --> 00:10:48,823
It's efficient, but it's also where these

338
00:10:48,823 --> 00:10:49,474
traps live.

339
00:10:49,823 --> 00:10:52,293
Even experts fall for them when they're

340
00:10:52,293 --> 00:10:54,003
tired, rushed or emotional.

341
00:10:54,183 --> 00:10:56,058
That's why stores play music,

342
00:10:56,058 --> 00:10:58,451
use countdown timers and put candy at

343
00:10:58,451 --> 00:10:59,291
the checkout.

344
00:10:59,471 --> 00:11:01,852
They're designed to catch system one when

345
00:11:01,852 --> 00:11:03,187
system two is off duty.

346
00:11:03,367 --> 00:11:05,189
Being smart doesn't protect you.

347
00:11:05,369 --> 00:11:06,635
Being prepared does.

348
00:11:06,984 --> 00:11:08,999
The solution isn't to try harder in

349
00:11:08,999 --> 00:11:09,748
every moment.

350
00:11:09,928 --> 00:11:12,795
Nobody can think carefully about every purchase.

351
00:11:12,975 --> 00:11:14,977
The solution is to make a few

352
00:11:14,977 --> 00:11:16,703
big decisions in advance,

353
00:11:16,703 --> 00:11:18,428
when system two is awake,

354
00:11:18,428 --> 00:11:20,568
and let systems carry them out.

355
00:11:20,748 --> 00:11:22,678
That's the theme you'll see again and

356
00:11:22,678 --> 00:11:23,825
again in the playbook.

357
00:11:24,175 --> 00:11:25,173
That's angle one.

358
00:11:25,353 --> 00:11:27,664
Anchoring, mental accounting,

359
00:11:27,664 --> 00:11:30,693
present bias, exponential growth bias,

360
00:11:30,693 --> 00:11:34,119
sunk costs, painless payments and the power

361
00:11:34,119 --> 00:11:34,757
of free.

362
00:11:34,937 --> 00:11:36,272
Now angle two.

363
00:11:36,452 --> 00:11:37,567
The psychology.

364
00:11:37,747 --> 00:11:39,610
These traps are more emotional,

365
00:11:39,610 --> 00:11:41,474
and in many ways more powerful.

366
00:11:41,823 --> 00:11:44,360
The emotional traps are about fear,

367
00:11:44,360 --> 00:11:46,317
shame, comparison and hope.

368
00:11:46,497 --> 00:11:48,589
They explain why people avoid looking at

369
00:11:48,589 --> 00:11:50,734
their accounts, keep up with friends they

370
00:11:50,734 --> 00:11:52,252
can't afford to keep up with,

371
00:11:52,252 --> 00:11:54,031
and expect things to get better on

372
00:11:54,031 --> 00:11:54,554
their own.

373
00:11:54,734 --> 00:11:55,985
They're deeply human,

374
00:11:55,985 --> 00:11:58,249
and recognizing them is the first step

375
00:11:58,249 --> 00:11:59,262
to escaping them.

376
00:11:59,611 --> 00:12:00,865
Trap number eight.

377
00:12:01,045 --> 00:12:02,264
The scarcity tunnel.

378
00:12:02,614 --> 00:12:04,145
In their book Scarcity,

379
00:12:04,145 --> 00:12:07,538
Sendhil Mullainathan and Eldar Shafir described how

380
00:12:07,538 --> 00:12:10,532
running short of money creates tunnel vision.

381
00:12:10,712 --> 00:12:13,208
When you're worried about this month's rent,

382
00:12:13,208 --> 00:12:15,533
it's very hard to think about retirement,

383
00:12:15,533 --> 00:12:16,610
or even next month.

384
00:12:16,790 --> 00:12:18,741
The urgent crowds out the important.

385
00:12:19,090 --> 00:12:21,118
This is why payday loans and high

386
00:12:21,118 --> 00:12:23,699
interest credit can seem reasonable in the

387
00:12:23,699 --> 00:12:24,129
moment.

388
00:12:24,309 --> 00:12:26,724
They solve the problem inside the tunnel,

389
00:12:26,724 --> 00:12:29,374
today's bill, while creating a bigger problem

390
00:12:29,374 --> 00:12:30,021
outside it.

391
00:12:30,201 --> 00:12:32,094
It's not a lack of intelligence.

392
00:12:32,274 --> 00:12:34,863
It's what scarcity does to anyone's thinking.

393
00:12:35,212 --> 00:12:35,932
Escape it.

394
00:12:36,112 --> 00:12:37,874
Build even a small buffer,

395
00:12:37,874 --> 00:12:39,364
a few hundred dollars,

396
00:12:39,364 --> 00:12:40,652
as fast as you can.

397
00:12:40,832 --> 00:12:42,799
Many people find that even a small

398
00:12:42,799 --> 00:12:44,303
cushion widens the tunnel.

399
00:12:44,483 --> 00:12:46,925
And make important long term decisions,

400
00:12:46,925 --> 00:12:49,491
like setting up retirement contributions,

401
00:12:49,491 --> 00:12:50,868
during calmer moments,

402
00:12:50,868 --> 00:12:53,748
so they run automatically when stress returns.

403
00:12:54,097 --> 00:12:55,479
Trap number nine.

404
00:12:55,659 --> 00:12:56,785
The ostrich effect.

405
00:12:56,965 --> 00:13:00,065
Avoiding information when you expect bad news.

406
00:13:00,415 --> 00:13:02,352
Researchers George Loewenstein,

407
00:13:02,352 --> 00:13:04,977
Duane Seppi and Niklas Karlsson found that

408
00:13:04,977 --> 00:13:07,663
investors logged in to check their accounts

409
00:13:07,663 --> 00:13:09,663
more often when markets were up,

410
00:13:09,663 --> 00:13:12,037
and less often when markets were down.

411
00:13:12,217 --> 00:13:14,674
People avoid information that might make them

412
00:13:14,674 --> 00:13:15,166
feel bad.

413
00:13:15,346 --> 00:13:18,091
With money, that can mean unopened bills,

414
00:13:18,091 --> 00:13:20,501
unchecked balances and ignored debt.

415
00:13:20,850 --> 00:13:22,990
The problem is that money problems grow

416
00:13:22,990 --> 00:13:23,648
in the dark.

417
00:13:23,828 --> 00:13:24,874
Fees pile up,

418
00:13:24,874 --> 00:13:26,562
fraud goes unnoticed,

419
00:13:26,562 --> 00:13:28,089
and debt compounds.

420
00:13:28,269 --> 00:13:30,286
By the time people finally look,

421
00:13:30,286 --> 00:13:31,988
the problem is much bigger,

422
00:13:31,988 --> 00:13:34,446
which makes the next look even scarier.

423
00:13:34,795 --> 00:13:35,550
Escape it.

424
00:13:35,730 --> 00:13:37,704
Make looking small and routine.

425
00:13:37,884 --> 00:13:40,062
Five minutes, once a week,

426
00:13:40,062 --> 00:13:42,156
same time, with a coffee.

427
00:13:42,336 --> 00:13:43,475
Open every account,

428
00:13:43,475 --> 00:13:44,553
read every number,

429
00:13:44,553 --> 00:13:45,332
and close it.

430
00:13:45,512 --> 00:13:47,311
Familiarity shrinks fear.

431
00:13:47,491 --> 00:13:48,760
After a few weeks,

432
00:13:48,760 --> 00:13:51,509
most people find the weekly check takes

433
00:13:51,509 --> 00:13:54,116
less time and causes far less anxiety

434
00:13:54,116 --> 00:13:55,456
than they expected.

435
00:13:55,805 --> 00:13:57,094
Trap number ten.

436
00:13:57,274 --> 00:13:58,458
Social comparison.

437
00:13:58,808 --> 00:14:00,863
We judge our lives by comparing them

438
00:14:00,863 --> 00:14:01,548
with others.

439
00:14:01,728 --> 00:14:04,325
Researchers at the Federal Reserve Bank of

440
00:14:04,325 --> 00:14:06,861
Philadelphia found that when someone in a

441
00:14:06,861 --> 00:14:08,654
neighborhood won the lottery,

442
00:14:08,654 --> 00:14:11,065
their neighbors were more likely to buy

443
00:14:11,065 --> 00:14:12,611
visible things like cars,

444
00:14:12,611 --> 00:14:15,023
and later more likely to face financial

445
00:14:15,023 --> 00:14:15,579
distress.

446
00:14:15,759 --> 00:14:17,721
Seeing others spend makes us spend.

447
00:14:18,071 --> 00:14:20,224
Social media turned this up to the

448
00:14:20,224 --> 00:14:20,730
maximum.

449
00:14:20,910 --> 00:14:23,317
You compare your everyday life with everyone

450
00:14:23,317 --> 00:14:24,521
else's highlight reel.

451
00:14:24,701 --> 00:14:26,919
The vacation, the new kitchen,

452
00:14:26,919 --> 00:14:27,510
the car.

453
00:14:27,690 --> 00:14:30,082
You never see their credit card statements.

454
00:14:30,262 --> 00:14:32,132
And comparison is a game you can't

455
00:14:32,132 --> 00:14:34,662
win, because there's always someone with more.

456
00:14:35,012 --> 00:14:35,825
Escape it.

457
00:14:36,005 --> 00:14:38,583
Compare yourself with your past self instead.

458
00:14:38,763 --> 00:14:40,434
Are you better off than last year?

459
00:14:40,614 --> 00:14:42,902
Unfollow accounts that make you feel poor.

460
00:14:43,082 --> 00:14:45,413
And remember, the people who look richest

461
00:14:45,413 --> 00:14:46,948
are often the most in debt.

462
00:14:47,297 --> 00:14:48,784
Trap number eleven.

463
00:14:48,964 --> 00:14:50,415
Hedonic adaptation.

464
00:14:50,595 --> 00:14:51,907
We get used to everything.

465
00:14:52,256 --> 00:14:54,581
A classic study by Philip Brickman and

466
00:14:54,581 --> 00:14:57,824
colleagues in nineteen seventy eight compared lottery

467
00:14:57,824 --> 00:15:00,332
winners with other people and found that,

468
00:15:00,332 --> 00:15:01,189
after a while,

469
00:15:01,189 --> 00:15:03,881
the winners were not dramatically happier in

470
00:15:03,881 --> 00:15:04,737
everyday life.

471
00:15:04,917 --> 00:15:07,924
Psychologists call this hedonic adaptation.

472
00:15:08,104 --> 00:15:10,140
We quickly get used to new things,

473
00:15:10,140 --> 00:15:11,935
and they stop making us happy.

474
00:15:12,285 --> 00:15:13,750
That's why the new car,

475
00:15:13,750 --> 00:15:16,169
the bigger house or the upgraded phone

476
00:15:16,169 --> 00:15:18,078
feels amazing for a few weeks,

477
00:15:18,078 --> 00:15:19,797
and then just feels normal.

478
00:15:19,977 --> 00:15:21,832
And then we want the next upgrade

479
00:15:21,832 --> 00:15:23,181
to recapture the thrill.

480
00:15:23,361 --> 00:15:26,542
That cycle, sometimes called the hedonic treadmill,

481
00:15:26,542 --> 00:15:29,411
keeps people spending more without feeling any

482
00:15:29,411 --> 00:15:29,909
happier.

483
00:15:30,259 --> 00:15:31,014
Escape it.

484
00:15:31,194 --> 00:15:32,297
Notice the fade.

485
00:15:32,477 --> 00:15:34,288
After your next big purchase,

486
00:15:34,288 --> 00:15:36,599
check in with yourself a month later.

487
00:15:36,779 --> 00:15:38,125
Is it still exciting?

488
00:15:38,305 --> 00:15:39,977
Use that knowledge next time.

489
00:15:40,157 --> 00:15:42,653
And spend more on experiences and time

490
00:15:42,653 --> 00:15:45,610
with people, which research suggests tends to

491
00:15:45,610 --> 00:15:47,843
bring longer lasting satisfaction.

492
00:15:48,192 --> 00:15:49,934
Let me pause here for a moment.

493
00:15:50,114 --> 00:15:52,357
If this is helping you understand your

494
00:15:52,357 --> 00:15:53,479
own money behavior,

495
00:15:53,479 --> 00:15:55,664
please subscribe to Ryan Rover Money.

496
00:15:55,844 --> 00:15:57,946
It's free and it helps more people

497
00:15:57,946 --> 00:15:59,060
find these videos.

498
00:15:59,240 --> 00:16:00,522
Okay, trap twelve,

499
00:16:00,522 --> 00:16:03,301
which explains why so many people never

500
00:16:03,301 --> 00:16:04,940
change anything at all.

501
00:16:05,289 --> 00:16:06,787
Trap number twelve.

502
00:16:06,967 --> 00:16:08,442
Status quo bias.

503
00:16:08,622 --> 00:16:11,071
We stick with whatever we already have.

504
00:16:11,421 --> 00:16:13,622
People tend to stay with their current

505
00:16:13,622 --> 00:16:16,518
situation, even when switching would clearly help.

506
00:16:16,698 --> 00:16:18,475
The same bank with high fees.

507
00:16:18,655 --> 00:16:20,919
The same insurance company every year.

508
00:16:21,099 --> 00:16:22,968
The same expensive phone plan.

509
00:16:23,148 --> 00:16:25,595
Changing feels like effort and risk,

510
00:16:25,595 --> 00:16:26,410
so we don't.

511
00:16:26,760 --> 00:16:29,249
Brigitte Madrian and Dennis Shea studied a

512
00:16:29,249 --> 00:16:31,855
company that switched its retirement plan to

513
00:16:31,855 --> 00:16:33,100
automatic enrollment.

514
00:16:33,280 --> 00:16:36,571
Participation among new employees jumped dramatically,

515
00:16:36,571 --> 00:16:38,582
from well under half to the large

516
00:16:38,582 --> 00:16:39,131
majority.

517
00:16:39,311 --> 00:16:40,518
Nothing else changed.

518
00:16:40,698 --> 00:16:42,887
People simply stuck with the default,

519
00:16:42,887 --> 00:16:43,833
whatever it was.

520
00:16:44,183 --> 00:16:44,857
Escape it.

521
00:16:45,037 --> 00:16:47,626
Use status quo bias to your advantage.

522
00:16:47,806 --> 00:16:49,084
Set good defaults,

523
00:16:49,084 --> 00:16:52,705
like automatic savings and automatic bill payments,

524
00:16:52,705 --> 00:16:55,402
so doing nothing means doing the right

525
00:16:55,402 --> 00:16:55,828
thing.

526
00:16:56,008 --> 00:16:57,044
And once a year,

527
00:16:57,044 --> 00:16:58,144
review your bank,

528
00:16:58,144 --> 00:16:59,761
insurance and phone plan,

529
00:16:59,761 --> 00:17:02,220
and switch if there's a better option.

530
00:17:02,569 --> 00:17:04,241
Trap number thirteen.

531
00:17:04,421 --> 00:17:05,849
Optimism bias.

532
00:17:06,029 --> 00:17:08,630
Believing bad things happen to other people.

533
00:17:08,980 --> 00:17:12,324
Neuroscientist Tali Sharot and other researchers have

534
00:17:12,324 --> 00:17:15,417
documented that most people tend to underestimate

535
00:17:15,417 --> 00:17:17,184
their chances of bad events,

536
00:17:17,184 --> 00:17:18,068
like job loss,

537
00:17:18,068 --> 00:17:19,393
illness or accidents,

538
00:17:19,393 --> 00:17:22,296
and overestimate their chances of good events.

539
00:17:22,476 --> 00:17:24,148
Optimism helps us get out of bed

540
00:17:24,148 --> 00:17:24,932
in the morning,

541
00:17:24,932 --> 00:17:26,551
but it can leave us unprepared.

542
00:17:26,901 --> 00:17:29,742
With money, optimism bias is why people

543
00:17:29,742 --> 00:17:31,272
skip emergency funds,

544
00:17:31,272 --> 00:17:33,238
carry too little insurance,

545
00:17:33,238 --> 00:17:36,224
and assume their income will keep rising.

546
00:17:36,404 --> 00:17:38,448
Most of the time things work out.

547
00:17:38,628 --> 00:17:40,277
But the times they don't can be

548
00:17:40,277 --> 00:17:41,553
financially devastating.

549
00:17:41,903 --> 00:17:42,658
Escape it.

550
00:17:42,838 --> 00:17:44,835
Plan for the bad scenario while hoping

551
00:17:44,835 --> 00:17:45,729
for the good one.

552
00:17:45,909 --> 00:17:48,459
Build an emergency fund of three to

553
00:17:48,459 --> 00:17:50,135
six months of expenses.

554
00:17:50,315 --> 00:17:52,567
Check your insurance for major gaps.

555
00:17:52,747 --> 00:17:54,448
Then you can stay optimistic,

556
00:17:54,448 --> 00:17:56,149
with a safety net underneath.

557
00:17:56,500 --> 00:17:58,067
Trap number fourteen.

558
00:17:58,247 --> 00:17:59,269
Loss aversion.

559
00:17:59,449 --> 00:18:02,107
Losing hurts more than winning feels good.

560
00:18:02,458 --> 00:18:05,257
Kahneman and Tversky found that losses feel

561
00:18:05,257 --> 00:18:08,122
roughly twice as painful as equivalent gains

562
00:18:08,122 --> 00:18:08,773
feel good.

563
00:18:08,953 --> 00:18:11,210
Losing one hundred dollars hurts more than

564
00:18:11,210 --> 00:18:13,145
finding one hundred dollars pleases.

565
00:18:13,325 --> 00:18:15,791
This shapes many money decisions,

566
00:18:15,791 --> 00:18:17,585
often in unhelpful ways.

567
00:18:17,936 --> 00:18:20,588
Loss aversion can make people panic sell

568
00:18:20,588 --> 00:18:22,710
investments during market drops,

569
00:18:22,710 --> 00:18:23,903
locking in losses.

570
00:18:24,083 --> 00:18:26,388
It can make them avoid investing at

571
00:18:26,388 --> 00:18:28,891
all, keeping money in cash that slowly

572
00:18:28,891 --> 00:18:30,538
loses value to inflation.

573
00:18:30,718 --> 00:18:32,800
And it can make them hold losing

574
00:18:32,800 --> 00:18:34,167
investments too long,

575
00:18:34,167 --> 00:18:36,314
hoping to avoid admitting a loss.

576
00:18:36,664 --> 00:18:37,408
Escape it.

577
00:18:37,588 --> 00:18:40,107
Decide your plan before the market drops.

578
00:18:40,287 --> 00:18:42,100
Write down what you'll do in a

579
00:18:42,100 --> 00:18:44,579
crash, which for many long term investors

580
00:18:44,579 --> 00:18:45,848
is to keep investing.

581
00:18:46,028 --> 00:18:48,071
And stop checking investments daily.

582
00:18:48,251 --> 00:18:49,694
The more often you look,

583
00:18:49,694 --> 00:18:51,676
the more often you'll see losses.

584
00:18:52,026 --> 00:18:54,420
Underneath these traps are a few basic

585
00:18:54,420 --> 00:18:54,987
emotions.

586
00:18:55,167 --> 00:18:57,292
Fear, shame, envy and hope.

587
00:18:57,472 --> 00:18:59,318
Fear makes us avoid looking.

588
00:18:59,498 --> 00:19:01,286
Shame makes us hide problems.

589
00:19:01,466 --> 00:19:03,312
Envy makes us compare and spend.

590
00:19:03,492 --> 00:19:05,731
Hope makes us believe things will sort

591
00:19:05,731 --> 00:19:06,615
themselves out.

592
00:19:06,795 --> 00:19:08,652
None of these emotions are wrong.

593
00:19:08,832 --> 00:19:09,506
They're human.

594
00:19:09,686 --> 00:19:11,084
But left unexamined,

595
00:19:11,084 --> 00:19:12,623
they run our finances.

596
00:19:12,972 --> 00:19:16,471
Surveys by the American Psychological Association have

597
00:19:16,471 --> 00:19:18,868
consistently found money to be one of

598
00:19:18,868 --> 00:19:21,459
the top sources of stress for Americans.

599
00:19:21,639 --> 00:19:23,409
And stress, as we saw with the

600
00:19:23,409 --> 00:19:25,947
farmers study, makes good decisions harder.

601
00:19:26,127 --> 00:19:27,520
It becomes a cycle.

602
00:19:27,700 --> 00:19:29,699
Money worries create stress,

603
00:19:29,699 --> 00:19:31,985
stress leads to worse decisions,

604
00:19:31,985 --> 00:19:35,270
and worse decisions create more money worries.

605
00:19:35,619 --> 00:19:38,455
Breaking that cycle usually starts with one

606
00:19:38,455 --> 00:19:39,114
small win.

607
00:19:39,294 --> 00:19:40,745
Paying off one card.

608
00:19:40,925 --> 00:19:43,038
Saving the first five hundred dollars.

609
00:19:43,218 --> 00:19:45,122
Canceling three subscriptions.

610
00:19:45,302 --> 00:19:47,056
Small wins create relief,

611
00:19:47,056 --> 00:19:48,809
relief creates bandwidth,

612
00:19:48,809 --> 00:19:51,754
and bandwidth makes the next good decision

613
00:19:51,754 --> 00:19:52,245
easier.

614
00:19:52,425 --> 00:19:53,749
Momentum is real.

615
00:19:53,929 --> 00:19:56,306
Many people who turned their finances around

616
00:19:56,306 --> 00:19:58,306
say the hardest part wasn't the math.

617
00:19:58,486 --> 00:19:59,932
It was the first step,

618
00:19:59,932 --> 00:20:02,101
and the second one came much more

619
00:20:02,101 --> 00:20:02,561
easily.

620
00:20:02,910 --> 00:20:03,921
That's angle two.

621
00:20:04,101 --> 00:20:05,635
The scarcity tunnel,

622
00:20:05,635 --> 00:20:07,093
the ostrich effect,

623
00:20:07,093 --> 00:20:10,008
social comparison, hedonic adaptation,

624
00:20:10,008 --> 00:20:11,235
status quo bias,

625
00:20:11,235 --> 00:20:13,690
optimism bias and loss aversion.

626
00:20:13,870 --> 00:20:15,066
Now angle three.

627
00:20:15,246 --> 00:20:15,989
The playbook.

628
00:20:16,169 --> 00:20:18,379
Six traps about habits and identity,

629
00:20:18,379 --> 00:20:20,773
and the research backed tools that help

630
00:20:20,773 --> 00:20:21,939
people escape them.

631
00:20:22,289 --> 00:20:24,098
The good news about mental traps is

632
00:20:24,098 --> 00:20:25,389
that they're predictable.

633
00:20:25,569 --> 00:20:27,880
And anything predictable can be planned for.

634
00:20:28,060 --> 00:20:31,763
Behavioral economists haven't just discovered the traps.

635
00:20:31,943 --> 00:20:34,326
They've also tested tools that help people

636
00:20:34,326 --> 00:20:34,950
avoid them.

637
00:20:35,130 --> 00:20:37,510
This last group pairs each trap with

638
00:20:37,510 --> 00:20:38,369
its antidote.

639
00:20:38,720 --> 00:20:40,391
Trap number fifteen.

640
00:20:40,571 --> 00:20:41,639
Decision fatigue.

641
00:20:41,990 --> 00:20:44,177
Every day you make hundreds of decisions,

642
00:20:44,177 --> 00:20:46,310
and many people find that their judgment

643
00:20:46,310 --> 00:20:47,911
gets worse as the day goes on.

644
00:20:48,091 --> 00:20:50,871
That's one reason late night online shopping

645
00:20:50,871 --> 00:20:52,072
can be so tempting,

646
00:20:52,072 --> 00:20:54,537
and why people tend to grab convenience

647
00:20:54,537 --> 00:20:55,927
food after a long day.

648
00:20:56,107 --> 00:20:58,886
Research on decision fatigue is debated,

649
00:20:58,886 --> 00:21:01,457
but the experience of being too tired

650
00:21:01,457 --> 00:21:03,957
to choose well is familiar to almost

651
00:21:03,957 --> 00:21:04,583
everyone.

652
00:21:04,933 --> 00:21:07,214
Stores and apps make it worse with

653
00:21:07,214 --> 00:21:08,288
endless options.

654
00:21:08,468 --> 00:21:11,076
Research by Sheena Iyengar and Mark Lepper

655
00:21:11,076 --> 00:21:13,559
suggested that too many choices can make

656
00:21:13,559 --> 00:21:15,795
people less likely to choose at all,

657
00:21:15,795 --> 00:21:18,278
or less satisfied with what they choose.

658
00:21:18,458 --> 00:21:20,779
With money, too many options can lead

659
00:21:20,779 --> 00:21:21,845
to doing nothing,

660
00:21:21,845 --> 00:21:22,975
or choosing badly.

661
00:21:23,325 --> 00:21:24,068
Escape it.

662
00:21:24,248 --> 00:21:25,536
Reduce decisions.

663
00:21:25,716 --> 00:21:27,251
Plan meals for the week,

664
00:21:27,251 --> 00:21:29,362
so dinner isn't a nightly choice.

665
00:21:29,542 --> 00:21:31,434
Choose a simple investment,

666
00:21:31,434 --> 00:21:33,676
like a broad low cost index fund

667
00:21:33,676 --> 00:21:35,077
or target date fund,

668
00:21:35,077 --> 00:21:37,460
so you're not constantly deciding.

669
00:21:37,640 --> 00:21:39,463
Avoid shopping when you're tired.

670
00:21:39,813 --> 00:21:41,345
Trap number sixteen.

671
00:21:41,525 --> 00:21:43,917
Relying on willpower instead of commitment.

672
00:21:44,267 --> 00:21:47,289
Richard Thaler and Shlomo Benartzi designed a

673
00:21:47,289 --> 00:21:49,572
program called Save More Tomorrow.

674
00:21:49,752 --> 00:21:52,458
Employees agreed in advance to increase their

675
00:21:52,458 --> 00:21:54,983
retirement savings rate each time they got

676
00:21:54,983 --> 00:21:55,464
a raise.

677
00:21:55,644 --> 00:21:58,026
Because the increase happened in the future,

678
00:21:58,026 --> 00:21:59,649
and came out of a raise rather

679
00:21:59,649 --> 00:22:00,894
than their current pay,

680
00:22:00,894 --> 00:22:02,355
it didn't feel like a loss.

681
00:22:02,535 --> 00:22:04,469
In the first company to use it,

682
00:22:04,469 --> 00:22:07,588
participants' average savings rate rose from about

683
00:22:07,588 --> 00:22:09,584
three and a half percent to over

684
00:22:09,584 --> 00:22:11,893
thirteen percent in under four years.

685
00:22:12,243 --> 00:22:13,752
That's a commitment device.

686
00:22:13,932 --> 00:22:16,469
A decision made in advance that binds

687
00:22:16,469 --> 00:22:17,635
your future self.

688
00:22:17,815 --> 00:22:20,757
People who rely on willpower alone tend

689
00:22:20,757 --> 00:22:24,452
to struggle, because willpower is weakest exactly

690
00:22:24,452 --> 00:22:26,639
when temptation is strongest.

691
00:22:26,819 --> 00:22:29,650
Commitment devices remove the need for willpower

692
00:22:29,650 --> 00:22:30,476
in the moment.

693
00:22:30,827 --> 00:22:31,616
Escape it.

694
00:22:31,796 --> 00:22:33,003
Commit in advance.

695
00:22:33,183 --> 00:22:35,953
Ask your employer whether your retirement plan

696
00:22:35,953 --> 00:22:38,000
offers automatic yearly increases,

697
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:39,023
and turn them on.

698
00:22:39,203 --> 00:22:41,399
Or set a calendar reminder to raise

699
00:22:41,399 --> 00:22:43,784
your savings rate by one percent every

700
00:22:43,784 --> 00:22:45,101
time you get a raise.

701
00:22:45,452 --> 00:22:47,158
Trap number seventeen.

702
00:22:47,338 --> 00:22:48,673
A broke identity.

703
00:22:49,024 --> 00:22:52,460
Many people carry a story about themselves.

704
00:22:52,640 --> 00:22:53,697
I'm bad with money.

705
00:22:53,877 --> 00:22:55,374
People like me don't invest.

706
00:22:55,554 --> 00:22:56,704
I'll never get ahead.

707
00:22:56,884 --> 00:22:59,923
Psychologists have long noted that beliefs about

708
00:22:59,923 --> 00:23:01,760
ourselves shape our behavior.

709
00:23:01,940 --> 00:23:03,990
If you believe you're bad with money,

710
00:23:03,990 --> 00:23:05,430
you're less likely to try,

711
00:23:05,430 --> 00:23:07,203
and more likely to give up after

712
00:23:07,203 --> 00:23:07,756
a setback.

713
00:23:08,107 --> 00:23:10,649
Identities often come from childhood.

714
00:23:10,829 --> 00:23:12,861
What your parents said about money,

715
00:23:12,861 --> 00:23:14,428
how your family handled it,

716
00:23:14,428 --> 00:23:16,112
and what happened around you.

717
00:23:16,292 --> 00:23:18,138
But identities aren't permanent.

718
00:23:18,318 --> 00:23:21,147
Many first generation millionaires grew up with

719
00:23:21,147 --> 00:23:23,133
very little and built a new story

720
00:23:23,133 --> 00:23:23,856
about money.

721
00:23:24,206 --> 00:23:24,914
Escape it.

722
00:23:25,094 --> 00:23:26,313
Change the sentence.

723
00:23:26,493 --> 00:23:28,417
Instead of I'm bad with money,

724
00:23:28,417 --> 00:23:30,533
try I'm learning to manage money.

725
00:23:30,713 --> 00:23:32,666
Then prove it to yourself with one

726
00:23:32,666 --> 00:23:33,987
small action this week.

727
00:23:34,167 --> 00:23:35,978
Identity follows behavior.

728
00:23:36,158 --> 00:23:37,751
Every small win is a vote for

729
00:23:37,751 --> 00:23:39,235
the person you're becoming.

730
00:23:39,585 --> 00:23:41,094
Trap number eighteen.

731
00:23:41,274 --> 00:23:42,539
Learned helplessness.

732
00:23:42,890 --> 00:23:46,945
Psychologist Martin Seligman described learned helplessness,

733
00:23:46,945 --> 00:23:50,392
when people who repeatedly experience setbacks they

734
00:23:50,392 --> 00:23:52,150
can't control stop trying,

735
00:23:52,150 --> 00:23:54,650
even when things become controllable.

736
00:23:54,830 --> 00:23:57,083
With money, after years of struggling,

737
00:23:57,083 --> 00:23:59,870
some people simply stop believing their actions

738
00:23:59,870 --> 00:24:00,937
make a difference.

739
00:24:01,288 --> 00:24:02,472
It's understandable.

740
00:24:02,652 --> 00:24:04,893
If every raise has been swallowed by

741
00:24:04,893 --> 00:24:07,570
rising costs, and every savings attempt has

742
00:24:07,570 --> 00:24:09,500
been wiped out by an emergency,

743
00:24:09,500 --> 00:24:11,243
giving up can feel rational.

744
00:24:11,423 --> 00:24:13,626
But it often keeps people stuck long

745
00:24:13,626 --> 00:24:15,707
after opportunities have improved.

746
00:24:16,058 --> 00:24:16,708
Escape it.

747
00:24:16,888 --> 00:24:19,395
Start with something you can fully control

748
00:24:19,395 --> 00:24:20,649
and complete quickly.

749
00:24:20,829 --> 00:24:22,466
Cancel one subscription.

750
00:24:22,646 --> 00:24:24,237
Call to remove one fee.

751
00:24:24,417 --> 00:24:25,787
Save twenty dollars.

752
00:24:25,967 --> 00:24:28,383
Each small success proves that your actions

753
00:24:28,383 --> 00:24:28,777
matter.

754
00:24:28,957 --> 00:24:31,673
That proof is the antidote to helplessness.

755
00:24:32,024 --> 00:24:33,428
Trap number nineteen.

756
00:24:33,608 --> 00:24:35,060
The planning fallacy.

757
00:24:35,410 --> 00:24:38,706
Kahneman and Tversky described the planning fallacy,

758
00:24:38,706 --> 00:24:41,560
our tendency to underestimate how long things

759
00:24:41,560 --> 00:24:43,842
will take and how much they'll cost,

760
00:24:43,842 --> 00:24:45,998
even when we've been wrong before.

761
00:24:46,178 --> 00:24:48,210
Renovations go over budget.

762
00:24:48,390 --> 00:24:50,259
Weddings cost more than planned.

763
00:24:50,439 --> 00:24:53,051
Monthly budgets forget irregular expenses.

764
00:24:53,401 --> 00:24:56,328
With money, the planning fallacy shows up

765
00:24:56,328 --> 00:24:58,684
in budgets that leave no room for

766
00:24:58,684 --> 00:25:01,111
irregular costs, like car repairs,

767
00:25:01,111 --> 00:25:04,395
gifts, medical bills and annual subscriptions.

768
00:25:04,575 --> 00:25:07,489
Those surprises aren't really surprises.

769
00:25:07,669 --> 00:25:10,036
They're just expenses that don't happen every

770
00:25:10,036 --> 00:25:10,351
month.

771
00:25:10,702 --> 00:25:11,363
Escape it.

772
00:25:11,543 --> 00:25:13,169
Add a buffer to every plan.

773
00:25:13,349 --> 00:25:15,888
Many people add ten to twenty percent

774
00:25:15,888 --> 00:25:17,192
to project budgets.

775
00:25:17,372 --> 00:25:19,077
And create sinking funds,

776
00:25:19,077 --> 00:25:22,691
separate savings for predictable irregular costs like

777
00:25:22,691 --> 00:25:26,033
car maintenance, holidays and insurance premiums.

778
00:25:26,383 --> 00:25:28,008
And trap number twenty.

779
00:25:28,188 --> 00:25:30,057
Struggling in silence.

780
00:25:30,408 --> 00:25:32,355
Money is still one of the most

781
00:25:32,355 --> 00:25:35,342
taboo topics in many families and friendships.

782
00:25:35,522 --> 00:25:37,367
People talk about health,

783
00:25:37,367 --> 00:25:39,360
relationships and politics,

784
00:25:39,360 --> 00:25:41,721
but not their debts or salaries.

785
00:25:41,901 --> 00:25:44,041
Shame keeps money problems hidden,

786
00:25:44,041 --> 00:25:46,557
and hidden problems are harder to solve.

787
00:25:46,907 --> 00:25:48,969
When people do talk about money,

788
00:25:48,969 --> 00:25:49,936
with a partner,

789
00:25:49,936 --> 00:25:52,128
a friend, a counselor or a support

790
00:25:52,128 --> 00:25:54,577
group, they often find others face the

791
00:25:54,577 --> 00:25:55,544
same struggles.

792
00:25:55,724 --> 00:25:58,081
Accountability also helps.

793
00:25:58,261 --> 00:26:00,219
Telling someone your goal makes you more

794
00:26:00,219 --> 00:26:01,442
likely to follow through.

795
00:26:01,793 --> 00:26:02,443
Escape it.

796
00:26:02,623 --> 00:26:04,477
Find one person you trust to talk

797
00:26:04,477 --> 00:26:05,096
money with.

798
00:26:05,276 --> 00:26:08,039
Share one goal and one struggle.

799
00:26:08,219 --> 00:26:10,121
If debt feels overwhelming,

800
00:26:10,121 --> 00:26:13,362
nonprofit credit counseling agencies can help.

801
00:26:13,542 --> 00:26:15,079
You don't have to figure it out

802
00:26:15,079 --> 00:26:15,376
alone.

803
00:26:15,727 --> 00:26:18,146
To see how these traps work together,

804
00:26:18,146 --> 00:26:20,763
let's follow one person through a single

805
00:26:20,763 --> 00:26:21,613
ordinary day.

806
00:26:21,963 --> 00:26:22,845
Meet Sam.

807
00:26:23,025 --> 00:26:24,043
In the morning,

808
00:26:24,043 --> 00:26:25,266
Sam gets an email.

809
00:26:25,446 --> 00:26:27,721
Flash sale, forty percent off,

810
00:26:27,721 --> 00:26:29,010
ends at midnight.

811
00:26:29,190 --> 00:26:31,473
The original price is crossed out,

812
00:26:31,473 --> 00:26:34,159
and the sale price suddenly looks cheap.

813
00:26:34,339 --> 00:26:37,079
That's anchoring and urgency working together.

814
00:26:37,259 --> 00:26:39,189
Sam adds a jacket to the cart,

815
00:26:39,189 --> 00:26:40,733
and then two more items,

816
00:26:40,733 --> 00:26:43,563
because shipping is free over fifty dollars.

817
00:26:43,743 --> 00:26:45,380
That's the power of free.

818
00:26:45,731 --> 00:26:47,685
At lunch, Sam taps a phone to

819
00:26:47,685 --> 00:26:49,707
pay for a meal that costs more

820
00:26:49,707 --> 00:26:50,583
than planned.

821
00:26:50,763 --> 00:26:52,485
It doesn't feel like spending,

822
00:26:52,485 --> 00:26:54,839
because there's no cash leaving a wallet.

823
00:26:55,019 --> 00:26:56,563
That's the pain of paying,

824
00:26:56,563 --> 00:26:58,107
or rather, the lack of it.

825
00:26:58,287 --> 00:27:01,654
Later, a coworker shows off new headphones.

826
00:27:01,834 --> 00:27:04,005
Sam starts browsing for a pair.

827
00:27:04,185 --> 00:27:05,613
That's social comparison.

828
00:27:05,963 --> 00:27:06,980
In the evening,

829
00:27:06,980 --> 00:27:09,016
Sam is tired after a long day.

830
00:27:09,196 --> 00:27:11,046
Cooking feels impossible,

831
00:27:11,046 --> 00:27:12,749
so dinner is delivered.

832
00:27:12,929 --> 00:27:14,415
That's decision fatigue.

833
00:27:14,595 --> 00:27:17,573
A notification says the investment account is

834
00:27:17,573 --> 00:27:18,565
down this week.

835
00:27:18,745 --> 00:27:20,080
Sam doesn't open it.

836
00:27:20,260 --> 00:27:21,665
That's the ostrich effect.

837
00:27:21,845 --> 00:27:22,968
And before bed,

838
00:27:22,968 --> 00:27:26,038
Sam thinks, I'll start saving next month,

839
00:27:26,038 --> 00:27:27,685
once things calm down.

840
00:27:27,865 --> 00:27:30,608
That's present bias and optimism bias in

841
00:27:30,608 --> 00:27:31,499
one sentence.

842
00:27:31,849 --> 00:27:34,147
Sam isn't reckless or foolish.

843
00:27:34,327 --> 00:27:36,278
Sam is completely normal.

844
00:27:36,458 --> 00:27:38,948
Every one of those choices felt reasonable

845
00:27:38,948 --> 00:27:39,778
in the moment.

846
00:27:39,958 --> 00:27:42,271
Now imagine that same day with a

847
00:27:42,271 --> 00:27:43,790
few systems in place.

848
00:27:43,970 --> 00:27:46,257
No saved card on shopping sites.

849
00:27:46,437 --> 00:27:48,979
Savings that move automatically on payday.

850
00:27:49,159 --> 00:27:50,518
A meal plan for the week.

851
00:27:50,698 --> 00:27:52,520
Investment alerts turned off.

852
00:27:52,700 --> 00:27:53,774
The same person,

853
00:27:53,774 --> 00:27:54,781
the same brain,

854
00:27:54,781 --> 00:27:56,659
and a very different result.

855
00:27:57,010 --> 00:27:58,391
Here's something worth knowing.

856
00:27:58,571 --> 00:28:00,866
Many of these traps are well understood

857
00:28:00,866 --> 00:28:02,867
by the companies that sell to you.

858
00:28:03,047 --> 00:28:06,073
Retailers, apps and lenders employ people who

859
00:28:06,073 --> 00:28:08,562
study consumer behavior for a living.

860
00:28:08,742 --> 00:28:10,480
That doesn't make them villains,

861
00:28:10,480 --> 00:28:12,327
but it does mean the playing field

862
00:28:12,327 --> 00:28:12,979
isn't level.

863
00:28:13,159 --> 00:28:15,263
Let's look at a few common designs,

864
00:28:15,263 --> 00:28:16,526
so you can spot them.

865
00:28:16,876 --> 00:28:20,235
Countdown timers and limited time offers create

866
00:28:20,235 --> 00:28:23,238
urgency, which pushes decisions into fast,

867
00:28:23,238 --> 00:28:24,596
emotional thinking.

868
00:28:24,776 --> 00:28:28,080
Subscription models rely on status quo bias,

869
00:28:28,080 --> 00:28:30,558
because many people never cancel.

870
00:28:30,738 --> 00:28:32,736
Free trials rely on the power of

871
00:28:32,736 --> 00:28:35,046
free and on us forgetting the renewal

872
00:28:35,046 --> 00:28:35,359
date.

873
00:28:35,539 --> 00:28:38,116
Decoy pricing places an expensive option next

874
00:28:38,116 --> 00:28:39,663
to the one they want you to

875
00:28:39,663 --> 00:28:41,840
buy, which makes it look reasonable by

876
00:28:41,840 --> 00:28:42,470
comparison.

877
00:28:42,650 --> 00:28:44,415
That's anchoring by design.

878
00:28:44,764 --> 00:28:47,133
Buy now pay later services split a

879
00:28:47,133 --> 00:28:49,153
purchase into small payments,

880
00:28:49,153 --> 00:28:51,591
which plays on present bias and the

881
00:28:51,591 --> 00:28:52,636
pain of paying.

882
00:28:52,816 --> 00:28:55,777
Loyalty points make spending feel like earning.

883
00:28:55,957 --> 00:28:58,387
And endless scrolling feeds are designed to

884
00:28:58,387 --> 00:28:59,347
keep you looking,

885
00:28:59,347 --> 00:29:01,382
which means seeing more ads and more

886
00:29:01,382 --> 00:29:02,795
things other people have.

887
00:29:02,975 --> 00:29:04,565
None of this is secret.

888
00:29:04,745 --> 00:29:07,102
It's simply how modern selling works.

889
00:29:07,452 --> 00:29:09,411
Once you recognize these designs,

890
00:29:09,411 --> 00:29:11,191
they lose much of their power.

891
00:29:11,371 --> 00:29:14,103
A countdown timer becomes a signal to

892
00:29:14,103 --> 00:29:15,876
slow down, not speed up.

893
00:29:16,056 --> 00:29:18,761
A free trial becomes a calendar reminder.

894
00:29:18,941 --> 00:29:21,156
A split payment becomes a total price

895
00:29:21,156 --> 00:29:22,354
you add up yourself.

896
00:29:22,534 --> 00:29:24,502
Awareness won't make you immune,

897
00:29:24,502 --> 00:29:26,593
but it gives your careful system a

898
00:29:26,593 --> 00:29:27,700
chance to step in.

899
00:29:28,050 --> 00:29:30,516
Let's pull everything together into a simple

900
00:29:30,516 --> 00:29:30,965
toolkit.

901
00:29:31,145 --> 00:29:33,332
Five tools that beat most of these

902
00:29:33,332 --> 00:29:34,233
traps at once.

903
00:29:34,582 --> 00:29:35,430
Tool one.

904
00:29:35,610 --> 00:29:36,191
Automate.

905
00:29:36,371 --> 00:29:40,544
Automatic savings, investments and bill payments beat

906
00:29:40,544 --> 00:29:43,851
present bias, status quo bias and decision

907
00:29:43,851 --> 00:29:45,426
fatigue in one move.

908
00:29:45,606 --> 00:29:46,338
Tool two.

909
00:29:46,518 --> 00:29:47,876
Add friction to spending.

910
00:29:48,056 --> 00:29:49,431
Delete saved cards,

911
00:29:49,431 --> 00:29:51,746
use cash for problem categories,

912
00:29:51,746 --> 00:29:54,423
and wait twenty four hours before non

913
00:29:54,423 --> 00:29:55,870
essential purchases.

914
00:29:56,050 --> 00:29:58,156
That beats the pain of paying,

915
00:29:58,156 --> 00:30:00,403
anchoring and the power of free.

916
00:30:00,754 --> 00:30:01,589
Tool three.

917
00:30:01,769 --> 00:30:02,814
Look regularly.

918
00:30:02,994 --> 00:30:05,794
A weekly five minute money check beats

919
00:30:05,794 --> 00:30:08,887
the ostrich effect and keeps optimism bias

920
00:30:08,887 --> 00:30:09,403
honest.

921
00:30:09,583 --> 00:30:10,373
Tool four.

922
00:30:10,553 --> 00:30:12,004
Decide in advance.

923
00:30:12,184 --> 00:30:14,835
Write down your plan for windfalls,

924
00:30:14,835 --> 00:30:16,654
raises and market drops.

925
00:30:16,834 --> 00:30:18,648
That beats mental accounting,

926
00:30:18,648 --> 00:30:21,025
loss aversion and the scarcity tunnel.

927
00:30:21,375 --> 00:30:22,628
And tool five.

928
00:30:22,808 --> 00:30:23,540
Talk about it.

929
00:30:23,720 --> 00:30:24,668
A money buddy,

930
00:30:24,668 --> 00:30:27,173
a partner or a counselor beats shame,

931
00:30:27,173 --> 00:30:30,152
learned helplessness and the broke identity.

932
00:30:30,332 --> 00:30:31,562
Five tools.

933
00:30:31,742 --> 00:30:32,694
Twenty traps.

934
00:30:32,874 --> 00:30:34,406
You don't have to win every battle

935
00:30:34,406 --> 00:30:35,127
with your brain.

936
00:30:35,307 --> 00:30:36,912
You just need a system that wins

937
00:30:36,912 --> 00:30:37,965
most of them for you.

938
00:30:38,316 --> 00:30:39,209
Question one.

939
00:30:39,389 --> 00:30:41,711
Are poor people just making bad choices?

940
00:30:41,891 --> 00:30:42,100
No.

941
00:30:42,280 --> 00:30:45,090
That's the key insight of scarcity research.

942
00:30:45,270 --> 00:30:47,844
Anyone, regardless of intelligence,

943
00:30:47,844 --> 00:30:51,152
tends to make worse decisions under financial

944
00:30:51,152 --> 00:30:51,667
stress.

945
00:30:51,847 --> 00:30:54,233
The choices are a symptom as much

946
00:30:54,233 --> 00:30:55,028
as a cause.

947
00:30:55,208 --> 00:30:57,664
That's why systems and buffers matter more

948
00:30:57,664 --> 00:30:58,482
than lectures.

949
00:30:58,833 --> 00:30:59,738
Question two.

950
00:30:59,918 --> 00:31:02,054
Which trap is the most expensive?

951
00:31:02,234 --> 00:31:03,263
For many people,

952
00:31:03,263 --> 00:31:06,093
the combination of present bias and painless

953
00:31:06,093 --> 00:31:08,987
payments, which leads to high interest credit

954
00:31:08,987 --> 00:31:09,630
card debt.

955
00:31:09,810 --> 00:31:12,828
Interest above twenty percent compounds quickly,

956
00:31:12,828 --> 00:31:16,224
and exponential growth bias makes people underestimate

957
00:31:16,224 --> 00:31:17,356
how fast it grows.

958
00:31:17,707 --> 00:31:18,809
Question three.

959
00:31:18,989 --> 00:31:21,023
Can I really change how my brain

960
00:31:21,023 --> 00:31:21,404
works?

961
00:31:21,584 --> 00:31:23,163
You don't need to change your brain.

962
00:31:23,343 --> 00:31:24,969
You need to change your environment.

963
00:31:25,149 --> 00:31:28,347
Automatic transfers, removed saved cards and a

964
00:31:28,347 --> 00:31:30,850
weekly check work with your brain as

965
00:31:30,850 --> 00:31:31,267
it is.

966
00:31:31,447 --> 00:31:32,829
That's why they're so effective.

967
00:31:33,179 --> 00:31:34,142
Question four.

968
00:31:34,322 --> 00:31:35,448
Where should I start?

969
00:31:35,628 --> 00:31:37,357
Pick the trap that cost you the

970
00:31:37,357 --> 00:31:38,194
most last year.

971
00:31:38,374 --> 00:31:39,473
For many people,

972
00:31:39,473 --> 00:31:41,671
that's debt or impulse spending.

973
00:31:41,851 --> 00:31:44,207
Choose one tool from the toolkit and

974
00:31:44,207 --> 00:31:45,450
apply it this week.

975
00:31:45,630 --> 00:31:46,977
Then add another next month.

976
00:31:47,328 --> 00:31:48,326
Question five.

977
00:31:48,506 --> 00:31:50,619
Do these traps affect rich people too?

978
00:31:50,799 --> 00:31:51,623
Absolutely.

979
00:31:51,803 --> 00:31:53,914
Wealthy people fall for anchoring,

980
00:31:53,914 --> 00:31:56,645
loss aversion and social comparison as well.

981
00:31:56,825 --> 00:31:59,101
The difference is that many have built

982
00:31:59,101 --> 00:32:02,097
systems, like automatic investing and advisors who

983
00:32:02,097 --> 00:32:04,973
enforce discipline, and they often have cushions

984
00:32:04,973 --> 00:32:06,891
that make mistakes less painful.

985
00:32:07,071 --> 00:32:08,665
Systems and buffers,

986
00:32:08,665 --> 00:32:10,658
not superhuman willpower.

987
00:32:11,008 --> 00:32:12,261
Question six.

988
00:32:12,441 --> 00:32:14,111
How do I help a partner or

989
00:32:14,111 --> 00:32:16,424
family member caught in these traps?

990
00:32:16,604 --> 00:32:18,227
Start with curiosity,

991
00:32:18,227 --> 00:32:19,309
not criticism.

992
00:32:19,489 --> 00:32:21,525
Share what you've learned about the traps

993
00:32:21,525 --> 00:32:23,263
as something that affects everyone,

994
00:32:23,263 --> 00:32:23,959
including you.

995
00:32:24,139 --> 00:32:26,536
Then suggest one system you could try

996
00:32:26,536 --> 00:32:28,998
together, like a weekly money check or

997
00:32:28,998 --> 00:32:30,164
automatic savings.

998
00:32:30,344 --> 00:32:32,625
People change more easily when they don't

999
00:32:32,625 --> 00:32:33,293
feel judged.

1000
00:32:33,643 --> 00:32:34,722
Question seven.

1001
00:32:34,902 --> 00:32:36,740
Is it bad to be optimistic about

1002
00:32:36,740 --> 00:32:37,085
money?

1003
00:32:37,265 --> 00:32:37,834
Not at all.

1004
00:32:38,014 --> 00:32:41,054
Optimism keeps people investing for the long

1005
00:32:41,054 --> 00:32:43,610
term and trying again after setbacks.

1006
00:32:43,790 --> 00:32:45,755
The trick is to pair optimism with

1007
00:32:45,755 --> 00:32:46,449
preparation.

1008
00:32:46,629 --> 00:32:49,080
Expect things to work out over time,

1009
00:32:49,080 --> 00:32:51,327
and still keep an emergency fund,

1010
00:32:51,327 --> 00:32:53,711
insurance and a plan for bad years.

1011
00:32:53,891 --> 00:32:56,584
Hopeful and prepared is the best combination.

1012
00:32:56,935 --> 00:32:57,817
Quick recap.

1013
00:32:57,997 --> 00:32:59,727
Angle one, the numbers.

1014
00:32:59,907 --> 00:33:02,098
Anchoring, mental accounting,

1015
00:33:02,098 --> 00:33:04,969
present bias, exponential growth bias,

1016
00:33:04,969 --> 00:33:07,312
sunk costs, the pain of paying,

1017
00:33:07,312 --> 00:33:08,974
and the power of free.

1018
00:33:09,325 --> 00:33:11,275
Angle two, the psychology.

1019
00:33:11,455 --> 00:33:13,025
The scarcity tunnel,

1020
00:33:13,025 --> 00:33:14,516
the ostrich effect,

1021
00:33:14,516 --> 00:33:17,498
social comparison, hedonic adaptation,

1022
00:33:17,498 --> 00:33:18,754
status quo bias,

1023
00:33:18,754 --> 00:33:21,265
optimism bias and loss aversion.

1024
00:33:21,616 --> 00:33:23,311
Angle three, the playbook.

1025
00:33:23,491 --> 00:33:26,950
Decision fatigue, relying on willpower instead of

1026
00:33:26,950 --> 00:33:28,998
commitment, a broke identity,

1027
00:33:28,998 --> 00:33:32,034
learned helplessness, the planning fallacy,

1028
00:33:32,034 --> 00:33:33,870
and struggling in silence.

1029
00:33:34,220 --> 00:33:36,275
Remember the farmers from the beginning.

1030
00:33:36,455 --> 00:33:38,555
Same people, same intelligence,

1031
00:33:38,555 --> 00:33:41,807
different results, just because of money stress.

1032
00:33:41,987 --> 00:33:44,111
Being broke isn't a character flaw.

1033
00:33:44,291 --> 00:33:46,557
It's often a brain doing exactly what

1034
00:33:46,557 --> 00:33:48,578
brains do, in a world designed to

1035
00:33:48,578 --> 00:33:49,864
take advantage of it.

1036
00:33:50,044 --> 00:33:51,332
Once you see the traps,

1037
00:33:51,332 --> 00:33:53,237
you can build systems around them.

1038
00:33:53,417 --> 00:33:54,859
If you want the full plan,

1039
00:33:54,859 --> 00:33:55,969
it's in my playbook,

1040
00:33:55,969 --> 00:33:57,411
linked in the description.

1041
00:33:57,591 --> 00:33:59,545
Tell me in the comments which trap

1042
00:33:59,545 --> 00:34:01,097
you recognized in yourself.

1043
00:34:01,277 --> 00:34:02,473
I'm Ryan Rover.

1044
00:34:02,653 --> 00:34:03,849
See you in the next one.
